<ArticleLayout
  title="CRM Prototype Studio"
  description="What changes when AI-assisted delivery is treated as a governed engineering workflow rather than a code-generation shortcut?"
  eyebrow="Architecture Lab · Experiment 01"
  published="September 2026"
>
  <p class="article-kicker">A prototype for moving from business requirement to reviewable solution artifacts across CRM, data and integration platforms — with deterministic generation, testing, version control and explicit human approval.</p>

  <section class="case-summary" aria-label="Case study at a glance">
    <div><span>What I built</span><strong>AI-assisted requirement-to-solution control plane</strong></div>
    <div><span>Architecture idea</span><strong>Govern the delivery workflow, not just the model</strong></div>
    <div><span>What changed</span><strong>AI became one bounded step inside an evidence-driven pipeline</strong></div>
    <div><span>Key lesson</span><strong>Trust comes from traceability, deterministic controls and explicit authority</strong></div>
  </section>

  <h2>The question</h2>
  <p>AI coding tools can generate impressive outputs quickly. In enterprise delivery, however, speed is rarely the only constraint. Requirements change, approvals matter, environments differ, deployment authority is controlled and every meaningful change needs evidence.</p>
  <p>That led to a more useful architecture question:</p>
  <blockquote>Can AI accelerate solution delivery without allowing the model to become the architect, the deployer and the control system at the same time?</blockquote>
  <p>I built the Prototype Studio to explore that boundary.</p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>The Studio takes a prepared or custom business requirement and turns it into a constrained, reviewable solution blueprint. That blueprint can then drive deterministic generation of architecture, traceability, data, CRM, integration and test artifacts.</p>
  <p>The important part is what the Studio does <em>not</em> do. The model does not receive unrestricted authority to write arbitrary code into production systems. It interprets the requirement and proposes a structured blueprint. Approved generators then create artifacts from known patterns. Tests validate them. Humans approve progression. Git records the evidence.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Prototype boundary</strong>
    <p>The experiment uses synthetic data and controlled test environments. It does not contact real customers or treat AI-generated output as production-authorised simply because generation succeeded.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The architecture in one view</h2>
  <p>The control point is the workflow around the model rather than the model itself.</p>

  <div class="architecture-diagram prototype-studio-diagram" role="img" aria-label="Business requirements are interpreted into a structured blueprint, approved by a human, converted into deterministic artifacts, validated across platforms and recorded as evidence before controlled execution can be considered">
